{"page_content": "XGBoost Notes\nPage 1\n XGBoost\n Practical Notes for ML / Coding Interview Revision\nCore idea: XGBoost is gradient boosting with a strong regularized objective, efficient tree construction, and\npractical engineering improvements.\nBest mental model\n  Build trees one after another.\n  Each new tree focuses on the errors left by the current model.\n  Use gradients (and, for the standard objective, second derivatives) to decide what correction is useful.\n  Control tree complexity and update size with regularization and learning rate.\n \nRoadmap: intuition -> boosting -> objective function -> tree split math -> regularization -> hyperparameters -> classification -> practical\nsklearn usage -> common mistakes -> interview cheat sheet.\n\nXGBoost Notes\nPage 2\n1. What problem does XGBoost solve?\nSuppose a model predicts badly on some training examples. Instead of rebuilding the whole model from scratch, boosting\nadds a new weak learner that tries to correct the current model.\nFor an input x, think of the final prediction as:\n F(x) = F_0(x) + eta * f_1(x) + eta * f_2(x) + ... + eta * f_M(x)\nHere F is the ensemble, f_t is the new tree at round t, and eta is the learning rate. Small updates usually make the model\nmore stable.\nWhy trees?\n  Trees naturally model nonlinear relationships and feature interactions.\n  They do not require feature scaling for the usual tree split logic.\n  They can capture threshold behavior such as age > 30 or income < 50k.\n \nGradient boosting intuition\nAt round t, the model has a current prediction. XGBoost computes how the loss changes around those predictions and\ntrains the next tree to move the model in a better direction.\nThe important distinction from random forests: random forests mainly reduce variance through averaging many\nlargely independent trees; boosting builds trees sequentially so later trees depend on earlier errors.\n2. Gradient Boosting: the essential mechanics\nLet the current model be F_(t-1)(x). Add a new tree f_t(x):\n F_t(x_i) = F_(t-1)(x_i) + eta * f_t(x_i)\nThe new tree is chosen to improve the loss over the training examples.\nFor intuition, if the current model underpredicts a target by a lot, the next tree should give that region a positive correction.\nIf it overpredicts, the correction should be negative.\nA useful conceptual loop\n Step\nWhat happens\n1\nStart with an initial prediction.\n2\nCompute the loss on all training examples.\n3\nCompute first-order gradient information; XGBoost also uses second-order information.\n4\nBuild a tree whose leaf values produce a useful correction.\n5\nMultiply the tree contribution by the learning rate.\n6\nAdd the tree to the ensemble and repeat.\nKey point: boosting is sequential. This is why training is less embarrassingly parallel across trees than bagging,\nalthough XGBoost still parallelizes many expensive operations inside tree construction.\n\nXGBoost Notes\nPage 3\n3. XGBoost objective function\nThe objective balances two things: fit the training data well and keep the trees from becoming unnecessarily complex.\n Objective = Sum_i loss(y_i, yhat_i) + Sum_t Omega(f_t)\nFor the standard tree model, a common regularization form is:\n Omega(f) = gamma * T + 0.5 * lambda * Sum_j w_j^2\nwhere T is the number of leaves and w_j is the score assigned to leaf j. This means adding leaves has a cost, and very\nlarge leaf weights are penalized.\nSecond-order approximation\nAround the current prediction yhat_i, XGBoost uses a second-order Taylor approximation of the loss. Define g_i as the first\nderivative and h_i as the second derivative.\n g_i = partial L / partial yhat_i\nh_i = partial^2 L / partial yhat_i^2\nFor a particular leaf j, let:\n G_j = Sum_(i in leaf j) g_i\nH_j = Sum_(i in leaf j) h_i\nThen the optimal leaf weight has the classic form:\n w_j* = - G_j / (H_j + lambda)\nInterpretation: choose a leaf value that pushes predictions in the direction that reduces the loss, while lambda\nstabilizes the update.\nThis second-order treatment is one reason XGBoost is more than just “train a tree on residuals.”\n4. How does XGBoost choose a split?\nA split divides examples into a left and right child. XGBoost compares the objective improvement from making the split.\nA commonly used regularized gain expression is proportional to:\n Gain = 0.5 * [ G_L^2/(H_L + lambda) + G_R^2/(H_R + lambda) - G_parent^2/(H_parent + lambda) ] - gamma\nA split is attractive when the children have much better gradient-based leaf scores than the parent, after accounting for the\ncomplexity penalty gamma.\nMental picture\n Before split\nAfter split\nOne leaf with aggregate G_parent, H_parent\nTwo leaves with (G_L, H_L) and (G_R, H_R)\nOne prediction value\nTwo optimized prediction values\nNo extra leaf cost\nPay gamma for the added leaf structure\nThis is why the tree grows according to loss reduction rather than simply choosing the feature with the largest\nraw correlation with y.", "metadata": {"doc_id": "f48d3ad290d5c2cc", "parent_id": "f48d3ad290d5c2cc_parent_0", "parent_index": 0, "source": "data\\uploads\\7a3b40d81a20_XGBoost_Notes.pdf", "filename": "7a3b40d81a20_XGBoost_Notes.pdf", "chapter_or_section": "Section 1", "subject_name": "", "subject_domain": "", "token_estimate": 1216}}